{"abstract":[{"text":"A harmony element can contain many stacked chords (e.g. V of II). A sequence of harmony-chord groups is used","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"for this type of secondary function, where V of II would be represented by a harmony-chord with a 5 numeral","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"followed by a harmony-chord with a 2 numeral.","type":"text"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlharmony\/chord-swift.property"]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"chord","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": [","kind":"text"},{"text":"Chord","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLHarmony\/Chord-swift.struct","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL10MXLHarmonyV5ChordV","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"]","kind":"text"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"text":"Discussion","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A root is a pitch name like C, D, E, while a numeral is a scale degree like 1, 2, 3. The root element is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"generally used with pop chord symbols, while the numeral element is generally used with classical functional","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"harmony and Nashville numbers. It is an either\/or choice to avoid data inconsistency. The function element,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"which represents Roman numerals with roman numeral text, has been deprecated as of MusicXML 4.0.","type":"text"}]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Maps to a nested MusicXML ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"<harmony-chord>"},{"text":" element.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"sections":[],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage"